  - implementation-drift
  - test-gap
---

## Issues

- [x] `src/util/scan/index.ts` — `extractSummary` was changed to extract the `intent` YAML field
  instead of the first non-heading body line, breaking two pre-existing `scan` tests
  ("extracts summary from first non-heading line", "truncates long summaries to ~80 chars")
  and the `discover` deep-scan test ("deep scan (with path) includes summaries and warnings",
  which writes a file with no frontmatter and expects the body content to appear as summary).
  The implementation now silently returns `""` for any spec that has no `intent` field, which
  means summaries are always empty for todo.aide, research.aide, and plan.aide files —
  breaking the "summaries extracted from file content" contract for deep mode.
  Traces to: desired — "A call with a path returns the ancestor chain FIRST — then the detailed
  subtree of that directory with summaries and anomaly warnings."
  Misalignment: implementation-drift

- [x] `src/tools/discover/buildAncestorChain/index.ts` lines 94–98 — status badge is nested
  inside `if (description)`, so a spec with `status: misaligned` but no `description` field
  renders as the path alone with no flag. An ancestor that has drifted but was written without
  a description becomes invisible as a misaligned node.
  Traces to: desired — "Specs with status: misaligned are surfaced inline with a visible flag
  so any agent walking the tree notices drift immediately, without needing to call a separate
  validation tool." / undesired — "Misalignment flags that block the pipeline or require
  immediate action" (it is the opposite problem here: the flag disappears entirely).
  Misalignment: implementation-drift

- [x] `src/tools/discover/buildAncestorChain/index.test.ts` — No test covers the edge case
  where a spec has `status: misaligned` (or `status: aligned`) but NO description field.
  The plan lists tests for badges with descriptions present and "no badge when status absent",
  but the description-absent + status-present case is not covered. This is the exact case
  that masks the bug in finding 2 above.
  Traces to: plan step 7a — "Shows [misaligned] badge after description when status is
  misaligned" and "Does not show [pending] or any badge when status is absent" — the inverse
  (status present, description absent) is unnamed but is the critical gap.
  Misalignment: test-gap

- [x] `src/index.ts` line 88 — `aide_validate` tool description does not mention the
  `missing-description` check added in this enhancement. An agent calling `aide_validate`
  to understand what it will detect sees: "orphaned specs, missing specs, naming conflicts,
  broken links, orphaned research" — no mention of missing descriptions. Plan step 5
  explicitly required updating the tool description to describe new behavior.
  Traces to: desired — "The tool's description and output shape teach agents the cascading
  intent protocol by example" (description of validate must reflect that missing-description
  is now detected); plan step 5 says "update the description string for aide_discover" but
  validate was also changed without updating its description.
  Misalignment: implementation-drift

## Retro

The three implementation-drift findings share a root cause: the enhancement touched multiple
files (scan, buildAncestorChain, validate, index.ts) but the plan's step-by-step checkboxes
did not include an explicit cross-cutting review step to verify that newly-modified logic
didn't silently break pre-existing contracts.

The scan summary regression (`extractSummary` change) is the highest-risk pattern: a
behavioral change to a shared utility was made to serve the new feature without auditing
which existing tests and consumers depend on the old behavior. A plan step of the form
"run the full test suite before marking each step done, not just at the end" would have
caught this at step time rather than only at QA time.

The status-badge-suppressed-without-description bug would have been caught by the test plan
if the plan had included a test for `status: misaligned` with no description. The test cases
specified in the plan covered the four cartesian pairs of `{description present/absent} ×
{status aligned/absent}` but omitted `{description absent} × {status set}` — the fifth pair.
Writing test cases from a matrix rather than a list of named cases would have surfaced the gap.

The validate description omission suggests the plan's step 5 ("update the MCP tool description
for aide_discover") was interpreted narrowly: the implementor updated the discover tool
description correctly but did not apply the same update discipline to validate when adding a
new check there. Plan steps that touch secondary tools should explicitly name every tool
description that needs updating as a consequence.
